<p>Taiwanese President Lai Ching-te said Sunday that Taiwan “will never be sacrificed or traded away” and said U.S. arms sales are a security commitment under U.S. law, responding to President Trump’s comments after a two-day summit with Chinese President Xi Jinping in Beijing. Lai said Taiwan would not give up its sovereignty, dignity or “free way of life” under pressure, while calling continued U.S. arms sales and security cooperation “key elements” for regional peace and stability. Trump said in a Fox News interview aired Friday that he had not approved a proposed $14 billion arms package for Taiwan, saying, “I may do it, I may not do it,” and calling the sale “a very good negotiating chip” in talks with Beijing. He also warned against a formal Taiwan independence move and said he wanted China and Taiwan to “cool down”.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/1KwCOZ7T2-m7VzmXCTbFpDlNU_xg2YBcoUu51-MdyDkEh3837D-8Rq1-H4jsrYRFCV9WbIOjd1r5OtJ8VTLyVKD_GS4okQ6flhFvCAq1u9a5Of_WYG7cWh4TnbPvR_4Ix-hBRUEPkn07PIQrTYICR94WKrAjqw-IJPb6n5blWm4FvvFhjMGnts1YUf6GmIBnxHCojxKjigRk3myEf251YMcucVn8fJckDcsG9wrI8GvsAPeoxw5LnG1r83Qe-aPgM3VlbsYBAw-j1G05Ug' alt='Taiwan President Lai Ching-te speaks during a press conference in Taipei on Feb. 3, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Pending package: The Trump administration approved an $11 billion Taiwan arms package in December, while a separate package worth about $14 billion still awaits approval.</li><li>China pressure: Lai said Beijing’s military activity in the Taiwan Strait and surrounding waters has made the issue an Indo-Pacific security challenge, not just a problem for Taiwan.</li><li>Policy concern: Politico reported that Trump’s discussion of Taiwan arms sales with Xi could raise questions under the Six Assurances, which include a Reagan-era pledge that Washington would not consult Beijing on arms sales to Taiwan.</li><li>Political setting: Lai made his remarks at a Taipei youth forum held as part of events marking the DPP’s 40th anniversary, linking them to the party’s 1999 “Resolution on Taiwan’s Future” and its “Four Commitments”.</li></ul><blockquote>Taiwan will never be sacrificed or traded away - President Lai Ching-te</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Lai Ching-te: Lai thanked Trump for what he called continued support for peace and stability in the Taiwan Strait since Trump’s first term, including increases in the scale and value of U.S. arms sales to Taiwan. (<a href='https://fortune.com/2026/05/17/taiwan-president-us-arms-sales-trump-china-bargaining-chip-deterrent-regional-conflict/'>Fortune</a>)</li><li>President Trump: Trump said he and Xi had talked extensively about Taiwan but said he made no commitment to Beijing on what Washington would do. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/05/17/greer-trump-taiwan-arms-china-00925454'>Politico</a>)</li><li>U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer: Greer said Trump was still “considering” how to approach the deal and was focused on ensuring that “nothing happens” in the Taiwan Strait. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/05/17/greer-trump-taiwan-arms-china-00925454'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Alexander Yui: Taiwan’s representative to the United States said Taiwan wants peace and stability, adding, “We’re not the ones creating all this trouble.” (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/taiwan-peace-stability-trouble-china-alexander-yui/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Robert Gates: The former U.S. defense secretary said it would be a mistake for Washington to change its carefully worded position on Taiwan. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/video/robert-gates-says-it-would-be-a-mistake-for-the-us-to-change-its-rhetoric-on-taiwan/'>CBS News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/taiwan-wont-be-sacrificed-us-arms-sales-a-commitment-president-responds-to-trump/article70990781.ece'>Taiwan won&#x27;t be sacrificed, U.S. arms sales a commitment: President responds to Tru